WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:04.526
The cost model says count pages.

00:00:04.526 --> 00:00:07.053
Now we use it.

00:00:07.053 --> 00:00:09.579
A billion-tuple Listens table.

00:00:09.579 --> 00:00:12.105
You want Mickey's three listens.

00:00:12.105 --> 00:00:14.632
Reeding every tuple costs millions of page reeds.

00:00:14.632 --> 00:00:17.158
Sorting first costs even more.

00:00:17.158 --> 00:00:19.684
There is a way to find the right page in one IO.

00:00:19.684 --> 00:00:22.210
Hashing.

00:00:22.210 --> 00:00:24.737
A hash function maps a key to a bucket id in constant time.

00:00:24.737 --> 00:00:27.263
Every database in the world relies on it.

00:00:27.263 --> 00:00:29.714
A hash function takes any input.

00:00:29.714 --> 00:00:32.165
A number.

00:00:32.165 --> 00:00:34.616
A string.

00:00:34.616 --> 00:00:37.067
An entire record.

00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:39.518
It produces a fixed-size bucket id in a fixed range.

00:00:39.518 --> 00:00:41.969
Same input, same bucket, always.

00:00:41.969 --> 00:00:44.420
It aims to spread keys evenly so each bucket stays small.

00:00:44.420 --> 00:00:46.871
It is not compression.

00:00:46.871 --> 00:00:49.322
You cannot turn the bucket id back into the key.

00:00:49.322 --> 00:00:52.516
The simplest hash for an integer is the modulo.

00:00:52.516 --> 00:00:55.710
Take the key.

00:00:55.710 --> 00:00:58.904
Divide by the number of buckets.

00:00:58.904 --> 00:01:02.098
Keep the remainder.

00:01:02.098 --> 00:01:05.292
That is the bucket.

00:01:05.292 --> 00:01:08.486
With ten buckets, forty-two lands in bucket two.

00:01:08.486 --> 00:01:11.680
One fifty-seven lands in bucket seven.

00:01:11.680 --> 00:01:14.874
Ten forty-two also lands in bucket two, a collision with forty-two, because they share a last digit.

00:01:14.874 --> 00:01:18.068
Production code mixes the bits first so keys that look alike still scatter.

00:01:18.068 --> 00:01:22.796
Strings are harder, because a hash works on numbers.

00:01:22.796 --> 00:01:27.523
So first you turn the string into one.

00:01:27.523 --> 00:01:32.251
Adding up the character codes works but collides badly, since reorderings sum the same.

00:01:32.251 --> 00:01:36.978
A polynomial rolling hash weights each character by a power of a prime, so order matters and keys spread evenly.

00:01:36.978 --> 00:01:41.706
Then modulo into a bucket, exactly like the integer case.

00:01:41.706 --> 00:01:45.620
Collisions are not rare, they are guaranteed.

00:01:45.620 --> 00:01:49.535
Twenty-three people in a room and two of them likely share a birthday.

00:01:49.535 --> 00:01:53.449
A table of B buckets sees a fifty percent chance of a collision after only about square-root-of-B inserts.

00:01:53.449 --> 00:01:57.363
Two standard fixes.

00:01:57.363 --> 00:02:01.277
Chaining hangs a short list at the bucket.

00:02:01.277 --> 00:02:05.192
Open addressing probes for the next empty slot.

00:02:05.192 --> 00:02:09.106
Keep the load factor under about three quarters and each lookup stays close to one IO.

00:02:09.106 --> 00:02:13.824
Hashing turns a key lookup into a single IO, no matter how big the table is.

00:02:13.824 --> 00:02:18.542
You will see it again in hash indexes, hash joins, hash partitioning, and Bloom filters.

00:02:18.542 --> 00:02:23.261
Every place a database asks where does this key live, a hash function is answering.

00:02:23.261 --> 00:02:27.979
Next, a structure that uses hashing to skip the disk reed entirely.

00:02:27.979 --> 00:02:32.697
Bloom filters.
